From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 09:41:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 145/184] Wake the drive at bring-up, so D: does not need the
 diagnostic

D: has only ever been readable after a person launched CDTEST and let
it reach round 3. That is not a quirk of the diagnostic: rounds 1 and 2
hand the disc to the parked firmware with DRV_INIT on, and round 3
takes it back and reads the filesystem with this driver. Round 3 alone
does nothing. Nothing on a normal boot calls DRV_INIT, and this drive
does not leave its no-information state without it, so at the desktop
the D: icon opens on an error no number of retries can clear.

Doom does exactly this and says so: three DRV_INIT sites in its sub
program, one of them the second instruction of its bring-up, followed
immediately by CDCSETMODE. The comment in this file claiming no Sega
read path calls DRV_INIT was wrong and the console had already said so.

So: a sector count of zero now asks segacd_bios_read for the wake and
not the read -- everything above brd_awake, which is DRV_INIT and the
wait for a state a read may start from, and nothing below it. cd_fetch
does the reading afterwards, as it does today.

It fires on the first block anyone asks for, which is the boot probe,
so the drive is awake before disk_init_all decides whether D: has a
filesystem. One visit, about two seconds on the console measured
between diagnostic rounds.

The cost when there is no disc: DRV_INIT is allowed twenty seconds
before it gives up, and the cartridge loader already spends up to
twenty of its own waiting for the drive, so an empty tray now delays
the desktop twice over. Worth fixing, not worth blocking this on.

Emulator: woke=1, cd_dtest 0x403 -- tried, succeeded, woken -- cd_why
0, header 00 23 25 01, and the status line reads DY at the desktop
with nothing launched by hand.
---
 bios/scdapi.h  | 10 +++++++++-
 bios/segacd.c  | 34 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 bios/segacd2.S | 15 +++++++++++++++
 3 files changed, 57 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/bios/scdapi.h b/bios/scdapi.h
index d44878c..a2e9d1c 100644
--- a/bios/scdapi.h
+++ b/bios/scdapi.h
@@ -28,7 +28,7 @@
 #define SCDAPI_H
 
 #define SCD_COOKIE   0x53674344L        /* 'SgCD' */
-#define SCD_VERSION  12
+#define SCD_VERSION  14
 
 /* Everything the disc diagnostic prints, in one go. Taken in a single
  * call rather than twenty accessors so that the numbers in one report
@@ -115,6 +115,14 @@ struct scd_snap {
      * contents question. Until that changes, cdd_ask_next() never
      * leaves cdd_confirm() and the whole probe rotation, disc length
      * included, has never run. */
+    /* Did bring-up get the parked firmware to wake the drive? 0 never
+     * asked (no BIOS parked), 1 DRV_INIT succeeded, 2 it was asked and
+     * would not. Nothing else on a normal boot calls DRV_INIT, and this
+     * drive does not leave its no-information state without it -- so
+     * before this existed, D: was readable only after a person launched
+     * the diagnostic and let it reach round 3. */
+    unsigned char  woke;
+
     unsigned char  cddtoc;              /* cdd_ok: two different subs echoed */
     unsigned char  cddsub;              /* the last nibble 1 seen */
 
diff --git a/bios/segacd.c b/bios/segacd.c
index 3c328ed..becf4ff 100644
--- a/bios/segacd.c
+++ b/bios/segacd.c
@@ -578,6 +578,9 @@ static BOOL cd_ring_find(ULONG lba, UWORD *pt)
  * disc through this exact path, and every read after that either works
  * or shows up in the error count beside it. */
 static UBYTE cd_dtest;
+static UBYTE cd_woke;           /* 1 the firmware woke the drive for us,
+                                 * 2 it was asked and would not */
+
 
 static UBYTE cdc_altctrl;       /* holding B picks the other CDC control pair;
                                  * declared here because the init that reads
@@ -1955,7 +1958,8 @@ static void cdr_report(void)
      * a live value, re-read every report. */
     CDREPORT[27] = (UWORD)(cd_dtest
                          | (cd_dead ? 0x100 : 0)
-                         | (cd_deadrun ? 0x200 : 0));
+                         | (cd_deadrun ? 0x200 : 0)
+                         | ((UWORD)cd_woke << 10));
     CDREPORT[28] = cd_why;
     /* The drive's own state, nibble 0 of its status packet. The
      * BIOS dispatches sixteen ways on this byte at $140E and this
@@ -2975,6 +2979,33 @@ static BOOL cd_read_block(ULONG block, UBYTE *buf)
             cd_ts = 1;
             cd_ts_cmdsave = cdd_cmd_on;
             cdd_cmd_on = 0;     /* our VBL stops talking to the drive */
+        } else if (segacd_bios_available()) {
+            /* Otherwise: let the firmware wake the drive, once, and
+             * then read it ourselves.
+             *
+             * This is the shape the console arrived at by hand and it
+             * took three rounds of a diagnostic to see it. Rounds 1 and
+             * 2 handed the disc to the parked firmware with DRV_INIT
+             * on; round 3 took it back and read the filesystem with
+             * this driver -- Getbpb, Dfree, Fsfirst, Fopen, Fread, all
+             * of them, on the first try. Round 3 alone does nothing,
+             * because nothing else on a normal boot ever calls
+             * DRV_INIT, and this drive does not leave its
+             * no-information state without it. So D: has only ever been
+             * readable after a person launched CDTEST.
+             *
+             * Doom does the same thing and says so plainly: three
+             * DRV_INIT sites in its sub program, one of them the second
+             * instruction of its bring-up. The note in this file
+             * claiming no Sega read path calls DRV_INIT was wrong.
+             *
+             * A count of zero asks segacd_bios_read for the wake and
+             * not the read. It costs one visit -- about two seconds on
+             * the console, measured between diagnostic rounds -- and it
+             * happens on the first block anyone asks for, which is the
+             * boot probe, so the drive is awake before disk_init_all
+             * decides whether D: has a filesystem. */
+            cd_woke = (UBYTE)(segacd_bios_read(0UL, 0) == 0 ? 1 : 2);
         }
     }
 
@@ -3604,6 +3635,7 @@ static void scd_snapshot(struct scd_snap *s)
     s->cddmask = cdd_statemask;
     s->cddfirst = cdd_first;
     s->cddftick = cdd_firsttick;
+    s->woke = cd_woke;
     s->cddtoc = cdd_ok;
     s->cddsub = cdd_stat[1];
 
diff --git a/bios/segacd2.S b/bios/segacd2.S
index 45aa3c1..9b3fb2e 100644
--- a/bios/segacd2.S
+++ b/bios/segacd2.S
@@ -612,6 +612,21 @@ brd_init_done:
         clr.l   _segacd_bios_frames
         move.b  #1,_bios_woken
 brd_awake:
+        // A count of zero means "wake the drive and nothing else".
+        //
+        // Everything above this line is the part D: actually needs from
+        // the firmware: DRV_INIT, which spins the disc up, reads the
+        // table of contents and leaves the drive in a state a read may
+        // start from. Everything below is a read this driver would
+        // rather perform itself, and does -- the console's round 3 is
+        // our own CDC path, and it works, but only because rounds 1 and
+        // 2 had run DRV_INIT first.
+        //
+        // Nothing on a normal boot ran it, which is why D: has been
+        // unusable until the diagnostic was launched by hand. Splitting
+        // the wake out of the read is what lets bring-up call it.
+        tst.l   4(a5)
+        beq     brd_done                // stage stays zero: woken, no read
 
         movea.l a5,a0
         move.w  #0x0089,d0              // CDCSTOP
